  "markdown": "## Business Model\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites globally, offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products in a wide range of categories [10-K line 00134]. The company’s model is based on high sales volumes and rapid inventory turnover, achieved through volume purchasing, efficient distribution, and reduced handling of merchandise in no-frills, self-service warehouse facilities [10-K line 00134]. Costco buys most merchandise directly from suppliers, routing it to cross-docking consolidation points or directly to warehouses, creating freight volume and handling efficiencies [10-K line 00135]. The company operates with shorter hours than many other retailers, resulting in lower labor costs relative to sales volume [10-K line 00140].\n\n## Competitive Position\nCostco competes globally with global, national, and regional wholesalers and retailers, including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, gasoline stations, hard discounters, department and specialty stores, and operators selling a single category or narrow range of merchandise [10-K line 00190]. Notable competitors in the U.S. include Walmart, Target, Kroger, and Amazon, as well as other warehouse clubs such as Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club [10-K line 00190]. The company's competitive advantage includes a strong brand identity, particularly its private-label Kirkland Signature products, which are perceived by members as high quality at lower prices than national brands [10-K line 00192]. Costco also leverages its membership format and loyalty to drive repeat visits and consistent fee revenue [10-K line 00167].\n\n## Growth Drivers\nKey growth drivers include increased comparable sales, driven by shopping frequency and average ticket size, as well as the opening of new warehouses [10-K line 00451]. The company’s membership base has grown consistently, with paid members reaching 84,100 thousand at the end of 2026 [10-K line 00161]. Executive membership penetration, which represented 75.2% of worldwide net sales in 2026, is another growth lever [10-K line 00166]. Additionally, digitally-enabled sales, representing 11% of total net sales in 2026, have grown at a faster pace than traditional sales [10-K line 00153].\n\n## Catalysts\nA key catalyst for growth has been the expansion of e-commerce operations and business centers, which complement warehouse operations [10-K line 00153]. The company also benefits from its strong employee engagement and retention, with a high U.S. and Canada retention rate of approximately 94% in 2026 [10-K line 00181]. Furthermore, Costco's ability to adapt pricing strategies and merchandise mix in response to cost increases and competition has historically supported profitability [10-K line 00399].\n\n## Recent Developments\nIn 2026, Costco opened 28 new warehouses (including three relocations), adding 25 net new warehouses [10-K line 00415]. Net sales rose 10% to $297.2 billion, with comparable sales increasing 8% [10-K line 00451]. Membership fees increased 11% to $5.9 billion, driven by new member sign-ups and fee increases [10-K line 00458]. The company reported a 14% increase in net income to $9.226 billion, or $20.76 per diluted share [10-K line 00422]. In April 2026, the Board of Directors approved a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend [10-K line 00423].\n\n## Material Risks\nCostco faces material risks including competition from other retailers and e-commerce platforms, which can impact pricing and margins [10-K line 00399]. Changes in foreign currency exchange rates and inflation or deflation in the cost of gasoline also affect net sales and gross margins [10-K line 00398]. Additionally, while Costco invests in employee compensation and benefits to retain talent, these costs are partially outside the company's control and may impact profitability [10-K line 00407]. Tariff impacts, such as those related to IEEPA tariffs, have been a factor but were partially mitigated through refunds received in 2026 [10-K line 00425].\n\n## Business Quality and Competitive Advantage\nCostco's business quality is underpinned by its strong brand identity, low margins, and operational efficiency [10-K line 00134]. The company’s competitive advantage lies in its membership model, which reinforces loyalty and generates consistent fee revenue, as well as its private-label Kirkland Signature products, which are a significant driver of margin expansion [10-K line 00192]. Additionally, the company’s focus on employee engagement, productivity, and retention contributes to operational excellence and low turnover rates [10-K line 00181].\n\n## Cyclical Exposure\nThe company's performance is somewhat cyclical due to its reliance on consumer spending patterns, particularly in the U.S. [10-K line 00398]. The business is also sensitive to fluctuations in commodity prices, such as gasoline, which can influence both sales and gross margins [10-K line 00404]. However, Costco’s membership structure and low-margin model help buffer some of these cyclicality effects by providing stable fee revenue and predictable consumer behavior [10-K line 00167].",
